            <div xml:id="d501">

                <pb facs="0505" n="493" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg493"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.24 Raw Materials/1518: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-02-24T12:00:00+01:00">February 24,
                            1941—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received February 24—9:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>691. Both prior and subsequent to the receipt of the Department’s 552, February
                    20, the Embassy obtained informally assurances that the 100% quota would be
                    maintained. Since agreement to ensure the maintenance of the quota seems to be
                    sufficiently general, both among the unofficial and official members of the
                    British and Dutch Governments, a prior meeting today could only have done more
                    harm than good. A luncheon would have been equally a tour de force since a
                    representative of the Embassy is lunching with the International Committee after
                    its meeting tomorrow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
